cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this idea or software, please cite it as below."
title: "Common Ground: a knowledge graph as a shared medium for communication between humans and language models"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: "Gray"
    given-names: "Nicholas"
    # orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0000-0000-0000"
version: 0.2.0
date-released: 2026-10-09
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/ndg13b/CommonGround"
abstract: >-
  A prototype in which humans and language models build a knowledge graph
  together, as a shared and inspectable record of their common ground. Every
  link carries a rationale and may only cite registered sources. Participants
  agree, disagree or express doubt with reasons, and raise questions
  separately from doubts. Agreement counts only from an independent
  participant, and models must explain theirs. Claims that were wrong are
  revised and kept on record; claims that were too broad are refined, and
  both versions stay. Multi-hop answers report their weakest link and any
  open questions, and a review queue directs human attention to the claims
  that current questions depend on.
keywords:
  - knowledge graph
  - large language models
  - human-AI collaboration
  - common ground
  - argumentation
  - retrieval-augmented generation
